34
00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:43.929
Jonathan Sobel: But the… The other big restriction was the one I'm going to talk about today.

35
00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:48.570
Jonathan Sobel: Which is, we had this… can you guys see the code here?

36
00:05:51.110 --> 00:06:04.270
Jonathan Sobel: the… we had this ability that we added at the end of June to have contract types, since I have a contract type T, and let's say I describe T as having a circuit C that takes a number as an argument.

37
00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:15.110
Jonathan Sobel: So we can say that in Compact, and we can say, hey, I'd like a circuit F that, given a value of type T, which means a contract that has a C circuit.

38
00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:26.009
Jonathan Sobel: I can take V and call it C circuit and pass it this argument. So this code is meaningful code and compiles and runs with the version of Compact delivered at the end of June.

39
00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:39.110
Jonathan Sobel: the big question is, what code gets run here at V.C? Where is… where does it get code for this? I have this value, V, of type T, where is the circuit code for that?

40
00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:48.760
Jonathan Sobel: And what we delivered at the end of June, we said the application can provide code for contract type T.

41
00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:53.249
Jonathan Sobel: And just one code for contract type T.

42
00:06:53.250 --> 00:07:13.089
Jonathan Sobel: And so it could say, here is the code you will run. Whenever anyone hands you a value of contract type T, then, and they say they want to run circuit C, here is the code for it. And so, therefore, this call would run the same code, no matter what specific V was passed.

43
00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:30.240
Jonathan Sobel: drill down on that a little bit, that means that if I had 5 different contracts, each of which had, you know, all kinds of different circuits, and all of them had a C of this signature, I could treat them all as type T, I could pass any of them to F,

44
00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:36.010
Jonathan Sobel: But my application gets to say, here is the C code you'll actually run. Now.

45
00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:39.340
Jonathan Sobel: if that C code isn't,

46
00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:59.479
Jonathan Sobel: isn't the code that was compiled with the particular contracts and deployed with them, then it would fail because the, the verifier key wouldn't match, right? I wouldn't be able to build this call and build the ZK proof for it and have it work. So, in fact, it's a little more constrained than I… than I said. It isn't really the application gets to say.

47
00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:05.749
Jonathan Sobel: here's some code C, trust me, it has to actually provide code that's gonna match what's deployed on-chain.

48
00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:13.719
Jonathan Sobel: Okay, but when you make that cross-contract call, the big constraint was you get one version of code for the type T.

49
00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:24.040
Jonathan Sobel: This code… this COIP is about relaxing that constraint to say, now you get what you would expect, which is different code for each value of type T.

50
00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:25.770
Jonathan Sobel: So if I call F,

51
00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:45.369
Jonathan Sobel: with, one contract, and it has one code for C, that's fine, and I call it with a different contract, it has different code for C, that's fine, and this V.C would run different code depending on the specific value V that landed there, which is more like what you would expect if you're familiar with OO or any kind of thing that does that kind of dynamic dispatch.

52
00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:57.199
Jonathan Sobel: Okay, so how does that… that's the goal of this. The motivation is go from type-based determination of what code to run in a cross-contract call to value-based determination.

53
00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:10.829
Jonathan Sobel: The way it worked before was that the code generated for this call site actually did an import of dot dot slash t slash contract slash index.js, so it was actually very static.

54
00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:17.750
Jonathan Sobel: It said, you know, your app needs to drop some code in at that path, and that is the code that will be run.

55
00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:19.460
Jonathan Sobel: And so there is…

56
00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:25.750
Jonathan Sobel: It was really not… it was very, very fixed, and even in terms of how you had to structure the app for it to work.

57
00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:30.070
Jonathan Sobel: So does everyone understand that situation and the problem that's being solved?

58
00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:35.770
Jonathan Sobel: I'll take that as a yes, if there's no… no complaints there.

59
00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:36.180
Joseph Denman: Yes.

60
00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:39.560
Jonathan Sobel: So, the,

61
00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:46.609
Jonathan Sobel: I'm gonna see if I can see more people, I just see one person, and that's just no fun to talk to. There we go, much better.

62
00:09:49.020 --> 00:10:04.369
Jonathan Sobel: So, the way that we saw this, we talked about different possibilities, and so let me actually skip down to the rejected ideas part, because it's kind of important, because there's a fairly obvious thing to do.

63
00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:13.090
Jonathan Sobel: The fairly obvious thing is have some kind of registry somewhere that says, oh, I can find the code for deployed contracts, I'll run it.

64
00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.649
Jonathan Sobel: In fact, maybe it would…

65
00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:31.869
Jonathan Sobel: be on-chain, it will just be deployed when you deploy the contract, we'll have the code to go with it, and when you say, run this circuit for this value, we go grab the code and run it. We rejected that for the time being, and there are two big problems and reasons we rejected that.

66
00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:41.149
Jonathan Sobel: One is we don't have any standardized representation of contracts yet, of compiled contracts, other than the JavaScript.

67
00:10:41.230 --> 00:10:54.989
Jonathan Sobel: Yes, we all recognize that's a shortcoming, and it's a thing we're working on, but at the moment, that's what we have. Which means that grabbing the code from just somewhere out there, even on-chain, would mean grab some JavaScript and run it.

68
00:10:55.080 --> 00:11:09.590
Jonathan Sobel: And even if you had code signing, you'd go, great, I'm grabbing the JavaScript that someone put there, and no one has swapped it with different code, but it still doesn't mean you can trust it, and it doesn't mean there's any kind of protection in terms of what secrets it can see that's in your app or anything.

69
00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:22.900
Jonathan Sobel: So that's, that is kind of unsafe. And there, while we've talked about, you know, there are things like sandboxed JavaScript, different things where you could try and do some kind of isolation,

70
00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:38.289
Jonathan Sobel: there isn't anything probably mature enough yet. We're investigating ways to do that. And furthermore, this is probably the most important point of why we didn't go ahead and try and do something that did some kind of sandboxed grab some code and run it, is that

71
00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:52.699
Jonathan Sobel: Which, if you move on to do a version of this that does support private state, where you can be doing a cross-contract call to something that has private state, then you really need a system that provides isolation of private state, too.

72
00:11:52.770 --> 00:12:11.489
Jonathan Sobel: And it would be a shame to deliver something that provided supposed isolation, and then have to throw it out and have a whole different approach to isolation when we handle private state. So for now, we are not delivering anything that says, just grab some code out there, magically built into the system, trust that it's okay, and run it.

73
00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:12.970
Jonathan Sobel: Instead.

74
00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:33.260
Jonathan Sobel: The thing that's being delivered is a way for the app itself, for the DAP to say, here is a lookup table. Let me go beyond that. Not really a lookup table, because it's more general than that. For the dApp to say, here is a way to resolve contract addresses to contract code.

75
00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:52.540
Jonathan Sobel: Right? And that could be as simple as a very static lookup table, or as complex as some full dynamic system that reaches out across the network and pulls things from a registry, whatever it wants to do, but that does leave it up to the DAP to decide how it wants to do this resolution from address to code.

76
00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:54.079
Jonathan Sobel: And…

77
00:12:54.080 --> 00:13:05.699
Jonathan Sobel: That means the DAP can decide to be very locked down and untrusting, or it could decide to be wide open and trusting, in which case it should tell its users it's doing that. But we aren't delivering a fixed solution for that.

78
00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:22.849
Jonathan Sobel: What we are delivering is the mechanisms that allow ADAPT to work this way. And that consists of a few crucial pieces. The first is a thing called the module type in JavaScript. Or in TypeScript, rather. A TypeScript type module.

79
00:13:23.150 --> 00:13:31.250
Jonathan Sobel: And a module represents, this module type represents a loaded contract code.

80
00:13:31.660 --> 00:13:41.219
Jonathan Sobel: Right? So, in particular, if you import the same thing that is imported by the currently generated code as of the end of June.

81
00:13:41.380 --> 00:14:00.589
Jonathan Sobel: it would actually produce a module, or rather, doing the import as promise of module, because, of course, import is, doing file I.O. or something there, so it comes with as a promise. But that means that there's, changes to how code is generated a little bit. So you can still do the import you used to do, and things will work.

82
00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:05.329
Jonathan Sobel: But part of what comes in in that import is a thing that represents this module.

83
00:14:06.030 --> 00:14:12.760
Jonathan Sobel: And so this… the module type represents compiled contracts.

84
00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:21.089
Jonathan Sobel: And then how do you resolve some contract address into that is there's a thing called a contract module provider.

85
00:14:21.190 --> 00:14:34.290
Jonathan Sobel: And it has one function, that's all it needs, this one method. A contract module provider is anything that has a resolve method that, given an address, a contract address, where that's a type defined by the on-chain runtime.

86
00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:41.370
Jonathan Sobel: It returns a module funk, that is, a thing that can be invoked to produce this promise for a module.

87
00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:49.270
Jonathan Sobel: So that's kind of all it takes, is an application says, here's how I want to resolve addresses.

88
00:14:49.400 --> 00:15:06.199
Jonathan Sobel: then, it can do whatever it wants to do to get the code for a particular contract address. Now, part of what's being delivered at the moment coming up, is, I think this is built into Midnight.js level, isn't it, Joe? The simple one.

89
00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:25.030
Jonathan Sobel: And so Midnight.js will come with a thing that says, here, let me build a contract module provider just from a map, the obvious, simplest thing that would work. Here's a map from contact addresses to module funks, and you could do it literally with the static, you know, sort of,

90
00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:28.120
Jonathan Sobel: Square bracket,

91
00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:45.689
Jonathan Sobel: thing that goes address to import, address to import, address to import, and each one of those imports would just be the code for the different contracts. And you would get a map, you can pass that kind of map to this builder, and you get a contract module provider that's the simple one, whose resolve just says, oh, that address, I know what the code is for that.

92
00:15:46.130 --> 00:16:05.139
Jonathan Sobel: But we also have tests that, some of our QA team has built inside Shielded already. Or is this, I think Matthew may have worked on this even, that does a much more sophisticated version that says, oh, when you call Resolve, let me do a lookup and dynamically load the code from some lookup system.

93
00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:23.479
Jonathan Sobel: That will go find the code for this address based on what's deployed, or based on some outside registry, and load that up. And so, that means we have the example of kind of the most static thing and a very dynamic lookup system, and you can imagine extending that to some truly external shared lookup if you wanted.

94
00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:30.309
Jonathan Sobel: That's up to the DAP to decide which one of those providers it wants to use, but it all works that way.

95
00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:33.580
Jonathan Sobel: That does come with it, some new kinds of error cases.

96
00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:45.110
Jonathan Sobel: So there's, a nicely defined… and Joe, I think, did a really beautiful job of coming up with the… a lot of interesting error cases that could happen here, and,

97
00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:46.979
Jonathan Sobel: Distinguishing between them.

98
00:16:47.020 --> 00:17:10.229
Jonathan Sobel: So that, you know, if you don't have a provider, that's a different thing from you have a provider, but it doesn't do the lookup properly, or maybe you tried to import some compact code, but it was compiled with the old compiler, and it doesn't have the right things to look like a module, that's a different thing. Or you have something that really is a module, and everything seems to work, except that the thing you're invoking expects

99
00:17:10.230 --> 00:17:13.530
Jonathan Sobel: To the code you loaded,

100
00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:28.249
Jonathan Sobel: has a particular verifier key associated with it, and this value that you're calling, when it's looked up on-chain has a different… has a verifier key associated with it, and they don't match, which means you've just provided code that doesn't match the deployed contract, and that will fail also.

101
00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:33.080
Jonathan Sobel: Because it's a, an implement… an implementation mismatch.

102
00:17:33.230 --> 00:17:48.439
Jonathan Sobel: And so, for all these different kinds of things, you get errors and some nice payloads with them, to say, oh, this one doesn't match. And one of the interesting things is there's no static way to guarantee, even in the June delivery, that

103
00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:59.340
Jonathan Sobel: Like I said, imagine you have 5 different contracts you've defined that have a C circuit, and you say, I'd like to pass these along as if it's a T, because Ts have a C circuit.

104
00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:10.480
Jonathan Sobel: Well, suppose it doesn't have the right number of arguments, or the right type. You could catch that statically, but you don't really know. You can fake it enough with JavaScript, it can't reliably be caught statically.

105
00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:22.209
Jonathan Sobel: And so you have to do dynamic checks on the argument counts and argument types and return types and all that. And there's nice error messages that say, oh, you passed a thing that dynamically doesn't match in some way.

106
00:18:23.050 --> 00:18:27.699
Jonathan Sobel: So, that is, what is being delivered with the next release of Compact.

107
00:18:27.740 --> 00:18:47.710
Jonathan Sobel: this way to do this sort of dynamic dispatch, which really takes you a long way towards being able to build, in Compaq something a lot like the DEXs that you have with Ethereum, because you can have these different implementations of things, and you can invoke one knowing its interface.

108
00:18:47.740 --> 00:18:59.540
Jonathan Sobel: in our case, a contract type, and get different behavior for the different ones. It does mean that the app is responsible for deciding how it wants to do the dispatch, but we have the mechanism in place.

109
00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:12.650
Jonathan Sobel: So, that's what I wanted to present. That's this… this Kope describes that, and gives the rationale, and, has some of that, explanation of why we didn't do the other thing yet. And,

